1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,999
Down 50%? Cool. Now you just need +100%.

2
00:00:04,000 --> 00:00:07,332
Start with $10,000. One bad stretch: $5,000.

3
00:00:07,333 --> 00:00:11,025
Now $5,000 has to do all the climbing.

4
00:00:11,026 --> 00:00:13,999
$5,000 back on $5,000? That's +100%.

5
00:00:14,000 --> 00:00:17,698
Small dips? The climb is barely longer.

6
00:00:17,699 --> 00:00:21,796
At −50%, the climb is double the fall.

7
00:00:21,797 --> 00:00:25,097
Past that, it gets ugly. Fast.

8
00:00:25,098 --> 00:00:27,999
−90% needs +900%. Bring snacks.

9
00:00:28,000 --> 00:00:31,497
Real life: the Nasdaq, 2000 to 2002.

10
00:00:31,498 --> 00:00:36,125
It fell 78% in about two and a half years.

11
00:00:36,126 --> 00:00:39,999
Getting back needed +353%. That took fifteen years.

12
00:00:40,000 --> 00:00:43,516
"Down 50%? I'll just make 50% back."
We've all thought this one.

13
00:00:43,517 --> 00:00:47,999
Nope. That still leaves you down 25%.

14
00:00:48,000 --> 00:00:52,203
Small pits, short climbs. Boring is the point.

15
00:00:52,204 --> 00:00:55,999
That's why this library starts with risk.

16
00:00:58,200 --> 00:01:01,949
Lore 001. Next: Lore 002, 35% winners can beat 60% winners
